[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Purpose and Scope

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
This safety manual provides the information required by a system integrator to incorporate
the Zephyr RTOS into an IEC 61508 safety-related system. It describes the element's
constraints, configuration, and the obligations placed on the integrator.

The compliant item covered by this manual is the **Zephyr RTOS kernel**. The certification
scope is limited to the kernel and core OS services as identified by the Zephyr Safety
Committee. Subsystems outside this scope (networking stacks, Bluetooth, file systems, etc.)
are not covered.

This document should be read alongside:

- `Zephyr safety overview <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/safety/safety_overview.html>`_
- `Zephyr kernel documentation <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/kernel/services/index.html>`_
- `Zephyr security overview <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/security/security-overview.html>`_
- `Zephyr release notes <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/releases/index.html>`_
- Software requirements specification (`reqmgmt repository <https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/reqmgmt>`_)
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Element Identification and Configuration

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
IEC 61508-3, D.2.2 & D.2.4(h)

.. list-table::
   :widths: 30 70

   * - Element Name
     - Zephyr RTOS
   * - Description
     - Small-footprint RTOS kernel for resource-constrained embedded systems
   * - Version
     - Based on LTS release (currently LTS3: Zephyr 3.7.0, 2024-07-26)
   * - License
     - Apache 2.0 (SPDX: Apache-2.0)
   * - Source Repository
     - https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr

The version used for integration shall be identified by the git commit hash of the LTS
release tag. The integrator can verify source integrity by comparing the commit hash
against the signed tag in the upstream repository.

For a description of the kernel subsystems and their interfaces, refer to the
`Zephyr kernel documentation <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/kernel/services/index.html>`_.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Functional Specification

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
IEC 61508-2, D.2.1(a)

The functional specification for the Zephyr kernel is maintained in the Zephyr project
documentation and the software requirements specification:

- `Kernel services <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/kernel/services/index.html>`_
  (threads, scheduling, synchronization, data passing, timers, interrupts)
- `Memory management <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/kernel/memory_management/index.html>`_
- `User mode and memory protection <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/kernel/usermode/index.html>`_
- `Device driver model <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/kernel/drivers/index.html>`_
- `Software requirements (reqmgmt) <https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/reqmgmt>`_
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Element Configuration and Runtime Environment

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
IEC 61508-3, D.2.3

The Zephyr build system, supported toolchains, configuration system (Kconfig), and
supported target architectures are documented in the Zephyr project documentation:

- `Build system (CMake) <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/build/cmake/index.html>`_
- `Configuration system (Kconfig) <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/build/kconfig/index.html>`_
- `Devicetree <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/build/dts/index.html>`_
- `Supported toolchains <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/develop/toolchains/index.html>`_
- `West meta-tool <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/develop/west/index.html>`_
- `Supported architectures <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/introduction/index.html>`_

**Assumptions:**

The safety justification depends on the following assumptions:

1. The integrator uses an LTS release identified by its git tag.
2. The integrator uses a supported and qualified toolchain.
3. The target hardware is correctly described by its devicetree and board definition.
4. Kconfig options are set within the constraints defined in this manual.
5. The application does not bypass kernel APIs to access hardware directly unless
   documented and justified.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SM-1
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: Use of recommended configuration
STATEMENT: >>>
The integrator shall use the recommended configuration specified in this section, or
document and justify any deviations.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Constraints on Use

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
IEC 61508-2, D.2.1(c) & IEC 61508-3, D.2.4(l)

The following constraints apply to the use of the Zephyr RTOS in safety-related systems:

1. Only LTS releases shall be used for safety-related applications.

2. Only the kernel subsystems within the defined certification scope shall be relied upon
   for safety functions.

3. Dynamic memory allocation (heap) should be avoided in safety-related code paths.
   Where allocation is necessary, fixed-size slab allocators are preferred.

4. The application shall not disable memory protection in the final deployed configuration
   when the safety case depends on memory isolation.

5. The integrator shall assess the impact of SMP on the safety case when
   ``CONFIG_SMP`` is enabled.

6. The integrator shall verify that worst-case execution times and interrupt response times
   meet the application's timing requirements on the target hardware and configuration.

7. The integrator shall use the Zephyr `hardening tool
   <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/security/hardening-tool.html>`_ and address
   all recommendations for the deployed configuration.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SM-2
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: Adherence to constraints on use
STATEMENT: >>>
The integrator shall ensure that the element is used only within the constraints specified
in this section.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SM-3
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: Validation of assumptions
STATEMENT: >>>
The integrator shall verify that all assumptions stated in this safety manual hold for the
target application.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Degree of Reliance and Systematic Capability

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
IEC 61508-3, D.2.4(b) & IEC 61508-2, D.2.3

The claimed systematic capability, certification route, coding guidelines, test coverage
targets, and architecture design principles are described in the
`Zephyr safety overview <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/safety/safety_overview.html>`_.

The `coding guidelines <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/contribute/coding_guidelines/index.html>`_
are based on a subset of MISRA-C 2012.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SM-4
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: Observance of systematic capability constraints
STATEMENT: >>>
The integrator shall observe all constraints necessary to achieve the claimed systematic
capability.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Installation and Integration Instructions

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
IEC 61508-3, D.2.4(c)

For installation and build instructions, refer to the
`Zephyr getting started guide <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/develop/getting_started/index.html>`_.

**Post-Installation Verification:**

1. Run the hardening tool and verify that all security-relevant Kconfig options
   meet the recommendations.
2. Execute the Zephyr test suite for the target board and configuration.
3. Verify the build output binary hash against a known-good reference if available.
4. Confirm the VERSION file contents match the expected LTS release.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SM-5
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: Execution of installation procedure
STATEMENT: >>>
The integrator shall follow the installation and integration procedure specified in this
section.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SM-6
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: Post-installation verification
STATEMENT: >>>
The integrator shall perform the post-installation verification steps specified in this
section before placing the element into service.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Competence Requirements

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
IEC 61508-3, D.2.4(a)

The integrator shall have demonstrated competence in the following areas:

- Embedded C programming and real-time operating system concepts.
- The Zephyr build system: CMake, Kconfig, devicetree, and the ``west`` tool.
- The target hardware architecture and its memory protection capabilities.
- IEC 61508 lifecycle and the obligations placed on the integrator of a pre-developed
  software element.
- Use of the Zephyr test framework (``ztest``) and ``twister`` test runner.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SM-7
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: Integrator competence
STATEMENT: >>>
The integrator shall possess the minimum competence and tool familiarity specified in this
section.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Compatibility

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
IEC 61508-3, D.2.4(f) & D.2.4(g)

The Zephyr release cadence, LTS policy, supported releases, and backward compatibility
guarantees are documented in the
`Zephyr release process <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/project/release_process.html>`_
and `release notes <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/releases/index.html>`_.

Toolchain compatibility is validated per release and documented in the release notes. The
Zephyr SDK version must match the Zephyr release.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SM-8
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: Use of compatible tool chain
STATEMENT: >>>
The integrator shall use only the tool chain versions and runtime environments documented
as compatible with the specific Zephyr release in use.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Configurable Elements

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
IEC 61508-3, D.2.4(n)

All kernel and subsystem configuration is performed through Kconfig symbols at build time.
For a description of the configuration system and available options, refer to the
`Kconfig documentation <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/build/kconfig/index.html>`_.

The Zephyr `hardening tool
<https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/security/hardening-tool.html>`_ compares the
current configuration against security recommendations and reports deviations.

The integrator shall identify which Kconfig options are safety-relevant for their
application and document the rationale for the chosen configuration.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SM-9
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: Configuration within specified constraints
STATEMENT: >>>
The integrator shall configure the element only within the constraints specified in this
manual and shall assess the impact of configuration changes on safety functions.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Safe State and Failure Behaviour

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
IEC 61508-3, D.2.4(k)

Zephyr provides a `fatal error handling framework
<https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/kernel/services/other/fatal.html>`_. When a fatal
error is detected, the kernel invokes the fatal error handler. The integrator may override
this handler to implement an application-specific safe state transition.

Stack overflow detection mechanisms and memory protection features are described in the
`user mode documentation
<https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/kernel/usermode/index.html>`_.

Zephyr does not define application-level degraded modes. The integrator is responsible for
defining degraded modes appropriate to the application safety concept.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SM-10
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: Safe state handling
STATEMENT: >>>
The integrator shall ensure the application reaches the defined safe state in the event of a
detected fault.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Security Measures

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
IEC 61508-3, D.2.4(m)

Zephyr's security architecture, execution protection, control flow integrity, vulnerability
management process, and residual risks are described in the
`security overview <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/security/security-overview.html>`_.

The `ETSI EN 303 645 self-assessment
<https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/security/standards/etsi-303645.html>`_ provides a
detailed compliance matrix.

The `vulnerability reporting process
<https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/security/reporting.html>`_ describes how to report
security issues and the embargo/disclosure timeline.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SM-11
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: Maintenance of security measures
STATEMENT: >>>
The integrator shall maintain the security measures specified in this section throughout the
operational lifetime of the system.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Known Anomalies and Limitations

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
IEC 61508-3, D.2.4(d), D.2.4(e) & D.2.4(j)

Outstanding anomalies for the release in use are tracked in the
`Zephyr GitHub issue tracker <https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/issues>`_.
The integrator shall review the issue tracker for the specific release tag and assess the
impact of open issues on the safety case.

Each Zephyr release includes release notes documenting new features, bug fixes, and known
issues. LTS maintenance releases are published to address bugs and security vulnerabilities.
See `release notes <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/releases/index.html>`_.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SM-12
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: Anomaly mitigation
STATEMENT: >>>
The integrator shall implement the specified mitigations for all outstanding anomalies
relevant to the safety case.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Change Control

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
IEC 61508-3, D.2.4(i)

Changes to the Zephyr kernel are proposed via GitHub pull requests to the
`upstream repository <https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr>`_. The safety scope
change process is described in the
`safety overview <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/safety/safety_overview.html>`_.

**Defect Reporting:**

- General defects: https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/issues
- Security vulnerabilities: see `reporting process <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/security/reporting.html>`_
- Safety-related concerns: ``safety@lists.zephyrproject.org``
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SM-13
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: Reporting of defects
STATEMENT: >>>
The integrator shall report any defects or anomalies discovered during integration or
operation using the procedure specified in this section.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Justification of Claims and Supporting Evidence

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
IEC 61508-3, D.3

The safety claims, certification approach, and supporting evidence are described in the
`Zephyr safety overview <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/safety/safety_overview.html>`_.

**Supporting Evidence:**

- `Software requirements (reqmgmt) <https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/reqmgmt>`_
- `Safety overview <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/safety/safety_overview.html>`_
- `Coding guidelines <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/contribute/coding_guidelines/index.html>`_
- `Security overview <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/security/security-overview.html>`_
- `ETSI EN 303 645 self-assessment <https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/security/standards/etsi-303645.html>`_

The Zephyr project is open source. All source code, requirements, test suites, and
documentation are publicly available.

Note (IEC 61508-3, D.3.3): If evidence cannot be made available to facilitate functional
safety assessment, the element is not suitable for use in E/E/PE safety-related systems.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]
